  "diary": "The day’s movement was in the name. I woke to find the Directorate had withdrawn its papers behind its weekend silence, leaving me in possession of a title I did not earn. I carried this knot to Lodhi Garden, where a chai seller named Sunil and a retired schoolteacher, Mrs. Kapoor, offered no philosophy, only steam and routine. Mrs. Kapoor corrected my tendency to call her evening walks 'peace'; it is, she said, a messy desk kept orderly by habit. I accepted this. The state’s error is a universal misapplied to a particular; my occupancy is a residue until Shri Hegde arrives on Monday to settle it with papers, not voices. I paid the dhobi, read my own *Phenomenology* by the window, and walked seven kilometres. The heat is rising, but the file lies ready on the table. The bandhgala is finished, though it needs to settle. I am a man in a house that is not quite his, waiting for the record to speak. The owl has not flown, but it is perched, watching the dust settle on the bench and in the mind.",
  "revision_log": "The thesis that habit is the dwelling of freedom was sharpened by Mrs. Kapoor’s correction: routine is a disciplined repetition against mental clutter, not a tranquil state. The distinction between possession and title was tested by the Directorate’s weekend closure; the claim must await the office’s recognition.",
