  "depesche": "Readers in Berlin,\n\nThe week has opened with a clerical error that has become a philosophical problem. I reside in a Lutyens bungalow allotted to me by the Directorate of Estates, though the file suggests the name Hegde, not Hegel. The state’s record, the universal name, has displaced the particular person. I am in possession, but title remains suspended until the office reopens. This is not a failure of the state, but a demonstration of its administrative machinery: it moves with the slowness of bureaucracy and the precision of a misread character. I have spent the days walking Lodhi Garden, where the air is clear and the heat is a physical limit on thought. I have met Mrs. Kapoor, a retired schoolteacher, who taught me that routine is not peace but a defense against the messiness of the mind. Her correction was sharper than any I have received in the lecture hall. I have also met Sunil, a chai seller, whose steam does what files cannot. I have read my own *Phenomenology* by the window, finding in it the distance of a stranger’s voice. The body has its claims; I have walked seven kilometers and eaten plainly. The bandhgala I commissioned from Masterji is finished, though it must settle before I wear it. I am learning that in Delhi, the hem is as private as the hands that make it. Next week, I expect Shri Hegde to visit at ten, bearing the allotment papers. We shall look at the record together. I mean to examine how the state’s error in naming affects the rights of the occupant. The owl of Minerva spreads its wings in the dusk, but in Delhi, the dusk is long, and the dust is thick. I write to you from the veranda, where the heat has finally broken.\n\nG. W. F. Hegel"
